      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(update-check): wrap malformed npm view JSON parse in try/catch",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\n`fetchNpmPackageTargetStatus` bounds `npm view --json` stdout to 1 MiB, but when the bounded string is malformed JSON the parse failure currently surfaces as a raw `SyntaxError` string through the outer catch. That gives callers no indication the failure came from npm metadata parsing.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nWrapped `JSON.parse` inside `parseNpmPackageTargetMetadata` with a contextual `try/catch`. The error is rethrown with the parse cause and is then caught by the existing `fetchNpmPackageTargetStatus` catch block, so the function returns `{ version: null, nodeEngine: null, error: ... }` with a clear \"npm view returned invalid JSON\" message.\n\n## User Impact\n\nUpdate/status checks report clearer diagnostics when npm returns malformed metadata; it is easier to distinguish an npm-side JSON glitch from other command failures.\n\n## Evidence\n\nTerminal proof (after fix) uses a real fake `npm` executable on disk:\n\n```bash\nnode_modules/.bin/tsx scripts/proof/update-check-malformed-npm-json.mts\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\n=== Proof: update-check malformed npm view JSON ===\n\nUsing fake npm executable: /tmp/openclaw-proof-npm-XXXXXX/npm\n\nResult: {\n  \"target\": \"openclaw\",\n  \"version\": null,\n  \"nodeEngine\": null,\n  \"error\": \"Error: npm view returned invalid JSON: SyntaxError: Unexpected token 'o', \\\"not valid json {\\\" is not valid JSON\"\n}\n\nPASS: malformed npm view JSON is caught and surfaced as an error.\n```\n\nRegression test:\n\n```bash\nnode scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/infra/update-check.test.ts\n```\n\nAll 32 tests pass.\n",
        "branch": "fix/update-check-malformed-npm-json",
